s a possible quadratic dependence between $x$ and $y?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083477 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3b02ef62-b97d-5ed4-8df8-3ed8bbb240cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083477 #edb/ref 1074 "In the case of quadratic dependence, the points on the plot should be scattered along a parabola.\n\nAmong the given options, the only scatter plot that fits the description is the following:\n\n![](images/fa/fa4b708819d3ab941b7a037150333b743dad9e26610d1223c0ad5d306a449f17.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083481 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186079781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083481 #edb/ref 1069 "q-10758" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083481 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083481 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/89/89aef4df3a3de7030d53a2fb2d6382087e9b744dbffa203fed521a230a00c6ba.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows a correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y.$ Which of the following is the most appropriate regression model for this data?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083481 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b78dcc81-fe97-5110-9ad6-126920439724" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083481 #edb/ref 1074 "It seems that the data points are scattered randomly. Therefore, there is no correlation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083482 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186079787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083482 #edb/ref 1069 "q-10760" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083482 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083482 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ce/ce59a60a1e32b0e7e98345d68a8267f1a89fbd49184fbe8e496ce08c6984263d.png)\n\nA company produces bicycles. The scatter plot above shows the correspondence between the number of bicycles made and the company's profit (in thousands of dollars).\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate regression model for this data?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083482 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1c12c46d-28d0-5df2-a339-acb942ffb937" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083482 #edb/ref 1074 "As values of $x$ increase, the corresponding values of $y$ first increase and then decrease. It seems like the points on the plot are scattered along a parabola, as shown below.\n\n![](images/20/20acefe1f03a1ba3dd7154eb0ec9ab462009ed1153d7edb282807fac57130328.png)\n\nTherefore, among the given options, the most appropriate regression model is *quadratic.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084129 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084129 #edb/ref 1061 20636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084129 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0db20ae8-4b57-5c01-a3c9-b037ecc1f9c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084129 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186084453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084130 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084130 #edb/ref 1061 20637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084130 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e9396b63-3259-5e91-b33f-bc8207544992" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084130 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084131 #edb/ref 1061 20638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084131 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0c6689f9-79f9-5e96-a3d8-4fa412cfbac6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084131 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084453 #edb/ref 1064 "A common problem in statistics is recognizing whether data follows a trend. Thankfully, we can often identify trends in data simply by looking at a scatter plot. We list a few common examples below:\n\n- **Positive linear correlation**: The points seem to follow a straight line with a positive slope.\n\n![](images/e5/e5b155a661e5cb548c30cc34af0933087a4b63b63295b4667757c355d1a32802.png)\n\n- **Negative linear correlation**: The points seem to follow a straight line with a negative slope.\n\n![](images/3d/3dc0506d5686bbc583a86960343a6d4019071edb45121a9688d4b482f16ac8f8.png)\n\n- **Quadratic correlation**: The points on the plot are scattered along a parabola.\n\n![](images/53/53ed6cecb371e0f31623803568d6e1d97057652f13156da9cc71a64f91beadb3.png)\n\n- **Exponential correlation**: The points on the plot are scattered along an exponential curve.\n\n![](images/29/2976d466ec40ff595f15f78b0a90e0f0ecc34c8c43c4f10179b7f124f73330f9.png)\n\n- **No correlation**: The points seem to be scattered randomly.\n\n![](images/eb/eb025d0075bf8c8f6ac236759fa786b3c7f145a8187e8c219f7fe0ee82d3a0f7.png)\n\nThe process of fitting data to a linear, quadratic, exponential, or another type of curve is called **regression**." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084453 #edb/ref 1065 3704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084453 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "41441cda-b0e7-53e7-ad07-57f8c12fdcfb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084453 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233106 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233106 #edb/ref 1069 "q-10764" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233106 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233106 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/5f/5f6bf9cffbcfb9cf223f42cc1430570ee311541b969176bc303b41e4668dcca8.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows a correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y.$ Which of the following is the most appropriate regression model for this data?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233106 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2762aa75-4e6b-57a7-8203-105f2612faf0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233106 #edb/ref 1074 "As values of $x$ increase, the corresponding values of $y$ decrease rapidly, too. It seems like the points on the plot are scattered along an exponential curve, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3f/3f9d9f1f29a67bb46f2623786420efda71e4250589170727929077decb47d5c4.png)\n\nTherefore, among the given options, the most appropriate regression model is *exponential.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545203 #edb/ref 1080 "quadratic" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545203 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545203 